// WHERE WE LOOKED AND FOUND NOTHING, WHICH IS A MEASUREMENT AND NOT AN ABSENCE.
//
// Two census lanes reached this need independently within an hour of each other. Southern New
// Jersey - Burlington, Camden, Gloucester, Atlantic, Cumberland, Salem, Cape May - yielded zero
// facility rows, and the Hudson Valley proper - Dutchess, Orange, Ulster, Putnam - yielded zero
// retail colocation. Both lanes wanted to record that as a RESULT and neither had anywhere to put
// it, so both wrote it into a pull-request body where the next reader will never find it.
//
// THE PROBLEM IS THAT A SURVEYED-EMPTY REGION AND AN UNSURVEYED ONE RENDER IDENTICALLY. A census
// with no row for Cape May County looks exactly the same whether somebody read every operator page
// in it and found no facility, or whether nobody has ever looked. The first is expensive knowledge
// worth keeping; the second is an open obligation. Collapsing them means the expensive half is
// re-derived by the next lane, and the open half is mistaken for settled - and this census has
// already made the second error once, when the absence of any New York row was reported as a
// consequence of a refused-operator ruling rather than as nobody having surveyed the state.
//
// This module is OURS and not an upstream fact. DESIGN section 3: observations this repository
// produces are receipts in the observing layer, never properties of the observed. So it lives
// under gunbc rather than extdeps, and it cites the facility modules rather than being cited by
// them.
//
// What a search actually resolved to, kept because a region is empty for reasons that differ in
// what they oblige next. A region full of aggregators needs a different follow-up from one where
// operators exist but sell no retail colocation.
type EmptyRegionCause
  = ResolvedToAggregatorsOnly { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | ResolvedToResellersWithoutFacility { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | OperatorsPresentButNotRetailColocation { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | DirectoryClaimContradictedByOperator { detail: NonEmptyStr }

// The three outcomes a region can have, and the two that must never be confused.
//
// SurveyedNoRetailOperator is the case where somebody looked, systematically, and there is nothing
// to model. Its searched list is what makes it a measurement rather than an assertion: a later
// reader can see the coverage and judge whether it was thorough, instead of taking "empty" on
// trust. RegionUnsurveyed is the case where nobody has looked, and it is the only arm carrying an
// open obligation.
//
// The per-arm notes were written between the arms, where section 4c does not model annotations -
// only module-item grain is captured, so an indented comment inside a declaration body is a parse
// error rather than a preserved rationale. They are hoisted here intact.
type RegionSurveyOutcome
  = FacilitiesModelled {
      facilities: List<ColoFacilityRow>
      densities: List<CabinetDensityClaim>
    }
  | SurveyedNoRetailOperator {
      searched: List<NonEmptyStr>
      causes: List<EmptyRegionCause>
      surveyed_on: NonEmptyStr
      surveyed_by: NonEmptyStr
    }
  | RegionUnsurveyed {
      obligation: NonEmptyStr
    }

type RegionCoverage {
  region_label: NonEmptyStr
  region_code: NonEmptyStr
  outcome: RegionSurveyOutcome
}

// THE SOLE AUTHORITY FOR "WAS THIS REGION SURVEYED", AND IT ANSWERS BY PRESENCE RATHER THAN BY A
// SECOND PREDICATE. A surveyed region contributes its facility count; an EMPTY surveyed region
// contributes zero and an UNSURVEYED one contributes nothing at all. The distinction is the whole
// point of the module, so the count is Optional rather than a zero that both arms could produce.
//
// A `coverage_is_surveyed(c) -> Bool` stood beside this and was deleted (review 62776): surveyed
// IFF the count is Present, so the Bool was a second representation of a constraint this function
// already carries, and DESIGN section 5 prefers the single authority over the check that restates
// it. The same PR had already deleted `cabinet_density_is_read` from the density carrier for
// exactly that reason - writing the predicate again one module later is the receipt that the first
// deletion was applied to an instance and not to the habit.
fn coverage_facility_count(c: RegionCoverage) -> Int? {
  match c.outcome {
    FacilitiesModelled { facilities, densities } => Present { value: facilities.length() },
    SurveyedNoRetailOperator { searched, causes, surveyed_on, surveyed_by } => Present { value: 0 },
    RegionUnsurveyed { obligation } => Absent,
  }
}

// RETRIEVAL COVERAGE AND DECISION UTILITY ARE SEPARATE FACTS, AND THIS FOLD COUNTS THE FIRST.
// Operator instruction, 2026-09-09: "Do not preserve density_claim_is_read := can make air ceiling."
//
// THE PREVIOUS DEFINITION ASKED cabinet_density_air_ceiling AND I DEFENDED IT, WRONGLY. The defence
// was that routing through the carrier keeps one authority for the word "read" rather than
// re-matching the coproduct here. That argument answers a different question than the instruction
// does. "Was a claim read from the operator" and "can this claim bound a cabinet" are not one
// question asked twice - they are two questions, and the carrier owns only the second. Deriving the
// first from the second silently makes coverage a function of usability.
//
// THE FOUR CASES THAT PROVE THEY COME APART, all from the operator and all real in this census: a
// verified "10kW+" LOWER bound was read and must not yield an at-most ceiling; a verified "2-16kW"
// INTERVAL was read and is not one ceiling point; a verified "3kW average" was read and does not
// bound an individual cabinet; a verified real-power claim with the COOLING MEDIUM unstated was read
// and must not yield an AIR ceiling. Under the old definition every one of those counts as unread.
//
// SO THE CENSUS WOULD HAVE APPEARED TO LOSE READINGS AT THE EXACT MOMENT IT GAINED THEM. Once the
// carrier can hold a figure's DIRECTION and its APPARENT-POWER unit, this corridor's seven Equinix kVA
// floors and every interval and average in the census become properly typed claims - and a
// ceiling-derived count would
// have reported that improvement as a drop to zero. A metric that falls when the data improves is
// measuring the wrong thing, and it would have been read as a regression by whoever saw it first.
//
// This is therefore a claim-PRESENCE question over the arms, which is what the coverage roster owns.
// It is not a second spelling of the ceiling: nothing here reads a figure, a unit, a direction or a
// cooling medium, and no Watt is touched. Requirement-specific usability stays where the operator put
// it, inside the selection projection, and cabinet_density_air_ceiling remains the sole authority for
// what a cabinet may draw - this module no longer calls it at all.
//
// AND THIS FUNCTION IS THE HABIT REPEATING, WHICH review 62935 CAUGHT AND THIS FILE ALREADY
// CONDEMNED IN ADVANCE. Nine lines above, coverage_facility_count carries the sentence "writing the
// predicate again one module later is the receipt that the first deletion was applied to an instance
// and not to the habit" - written about cabinet_density_is_read. density_claim_is_read asks the same
// question over the same coproduct, one module later, in the file carrying that sentence.
// DISPOSITION: YELLOW, not green.
//
// THE DIAGNOSIS ABOVE USED TO SAY THE DEFECT WAS "a Bool arm-matcher over a reusable coproduct". THAT
// IS WRONG AND IT IS WORTH CORRECTING IN PLACE, because it is the sentence a future reader inherits.
// The shape of an arm-membership question has THREE forms, not two, and the first repair of this note
// shipped the two-form version:
//
//   every arm nullary               -> collapse to an `_eq`. A complete inhabitant exists to name.
//   payload IS the answer           -> an Optional accessor RETURNING the payload. The very fact that
//                                      rules out equality - the arm carries fields - is the fact that
//                                      says return them.
//   the fact IS the discrimination  -> Bool, with the payload as EVIDENCE FOR the fact rather than the
//                                      value being requested.
//
// CabinetDensityClaim's arms both carry payload, so equality is out either way; the live question is
// the second branch against the third. This predicate is the third: "did this operator publish a
// density at all" is a COVERAGE fact, which is what a census roster counts, and the figures are what
// makes it true rather than what is being asked for. A caller wanting the figures asks the carrier's
// inventory route for them.
//
// SO THE DEFECT IS LOCATION, NOT SHAPE: the question has a home in the carrier and is asked again
// here. And the shape is not settled by "the arm carries fields" alone - the earlier wording of this
// note would have sent the next author to a Bool by default, and the one before that to an `_eq` that
// cannot be written for this type.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT REPAIRED IN THIS CHANGE. The right home is the carrier - a presence-shaped accessor
// that answers "was anything published" without also committing to a direction and a medium, which
// none of the carrier's direction-committed accessors can do. It cannot be written against today's
// arms, because the arms this predicate matches on are being replaced wholesale: DensityMarketedMax
// and DensityMarketedAirOnly dissolve into one DensityPublished carrying a non-empty list of
// figures, so an accessor authored now would match over a coproduct scheduled for deletion.
//
// TRIGGER, RESTATED AFTER READING DESIGN SECTION 4b(3) AGAINST IT - THE FIRST WORDING WAS THE EXACT
// DEFECT THAT RULE DESCRIBES. It read "gunbc#10885 landing". That named an ARTIFACT rather than a
// CAPABILITY, and section 4b(3) says in capitals that a trigger naming less than the capability it
// restores "will be satisfied while the capability stays dead". Both halves of that failed here, and
// checking rather than assuming is what found it:
//
//   - #10885 is CLOSED, superseded by #10888. A trigger naming a dead pull request can never be
//     satisfied by anything, so the obligation had no route to retirement at all.
//   - #10888 did NOT carry a presence accessor when the trigger was rewritten. Its carrier exposed
//     figure lookup, wording coverage and ceiling refusal, and no route answering mere publication.
//     So repointing the trigger at #10888 - the obvious repair, and the one about to be made - would
//     have fired on that merge and retired this obligation with the accessor still absent.
//
// THE CAPABILITY NOW EXISTS, AND STATING IT WITHOUT NAMING THE FUNCTION IS THE FOURTH REVISION OF
// THIS PARAGRAPH FOR THE SAME REASON. Three earlier wordings each named an artifact - a pull request,
// then a head SHA, then a function - and each was falsified within hours by that artifact moving. The
// carrier lane has since ruled one of those functions deleted as a duplicate, so a note naming it
// would have been wrong a fourth time, for a fourth reason, on a paragraph whose entire subject is
// that triggers must not name artifacts. Writing the artifact down was the defect each time; the
// lesson did not transfer until it had cost four edits.
//
// SO, NAMING NO ARTIFACT, AND NOT YET ON MAIN: the carrier lane's in-flight change to
// extdeps.colo.types carries a route from which "did this operator publish any figure" is answerable
// without committing to a medium or a direction. It is NOT on main, this module still matches arms
// locally, and the trigger is unmet until that route lands - review 62964 read an earlier present
// tense here as claiming otherwise, which it should not have. That holds whether the carrier
// exposes the question directly or exposes the figures and lets presence fall out - over a
// structurally non-empty figures carrier an empty result IS the unread arm, equal BY CONSTRUCTION and
// not by coincidence, so both shapes satisfy this trigger and neither is named here.
//
// THAT IS THE CAPABILITY-OVER-ARTIFACT RULE PAYING FOR ITSELF RATHER THAN BEING ARGUED FOR. The
// capability survived the deletion of the function implementing it. Every artifact-shaped wording of
// this same trigger has now been falsified - the closed pull request, the superseded SHA, the deleted
// accessor - while the capability sentence has needed no revision on the facts, only on the naming.
//
// WHAT MOVES ON REBASE IS THE IMPLEMENTATION SITE, NOT THE QUESTION, and review 62972 read the
// wording below as declaring a scaffold. It is worth separating, because section 6's scaffold test is
// "will this artifact survive the terminal architecture and be consumed by it" and the honest answer
// has two halves. The QUESTION - how many claims in this region were published - is permanent; it is
// the census's headline and the fold that asks it survives unchanged. This PREDICATE is the current
// implementation of it, and no other route exists in an accepted program today: the carrier route
// lives only on the carrier lane's unlanded head, so this is not a second path beside an existing
// modelled one, which is the parallel-authority tell section 6 actually names. When that route lands,
// the same fold calls it and this declaration goes. A migration whose destination is named and whose
// origin has no competing authority is not a throwaway artifact; it is one call site moving.
//
// ON REBASE this function is DELETED and coverage_density_read_count asks the carrier, in whatever
// shape the carrier settles on. It is NOT rewritten against the new arms: that would be the same
// predicate authored a fourth time in the file that already carries the sentence predicting the third.
//
// AND WHERE THE CARRIER ANSWERS WITH THE FIGURES RATHER THAN THE QUESTION, this fold destructures them
// rather than measuring them. `length(...) > 0` computes a TOTAL to decide a PRESENCE, which is the
// classic cost shape section 6 says to fix regardless of realized n, since n is not time-stable - and
// this fold ranges over the whole census where the carrier's own witnesses range over one or two
// claims. Matching the empty and non-empty arms answers it without a traversal. Stated here because
// the tempting spelling is the measuring one, and it is tempting precisely where the list is short.
// Recorded here rather than in a PR thread, because the next reader of this function needs to know it
// is owed a home, and needs the trigger to be one that can actually be checked.
fn density_claim_is_read(d: CabinetDensityClaim) -> Bool {
  match d {
    DensityMarketedMax { air_cooled, liquid_cooled, claim_wording, citation } => true,
    DensityMarketedAirOnly { air_cooled, claim_wording, citation } => true,
    DensityUnread { obligation } => false,
  }
}

// COUNTS DENSITY CLAIMS, NOT FACILITIES, and the two differ on purpose. Several corridor operators
// publish one density statement covering every site they run, so a claim is the grain at which the
// reading actually exists; attributing an operator-level claim to each of its facilities would
// invent a per-facility reading nobody published - the Summit withdrawal's error arriving by
// division instead of by proximity. Today the distinction changes nothing, because the single read
// claim is DataBank's and is facility-level over one site.
//
// THE DECISION THE NEXT LANE INHERITS, stated so it is met as a decision rather than as a latent
// fork. When a multi-site operator does publish a figure, a reader of densities cannot ask how many
// FACILITIES that claim covers, and the honest answer is that nobody knows unless the operator
// scoped it. The resolution is one level down, in the claim: were a CabinetDensityClaim to carry
// the facilities it was published FOR, facility-grain coverage would become derivable exactly where
// an operator stated the scope and would REFUSE where they did not - which is this module's own
// distinction, applied to the claim instead of to the region. Until then this count is claims, and
// a facility-grain number must not be manufactured from it.
fn coverage_density_read_count(c: RegionCoverage) -> Int? {
  match c.outcome {
    FacilitiesModelled { facilities, densities } => Present { value: densities.filter(d => density_claim_is_read(d: d)).length() },
    SurveyedNoRetailOperator { searched, causes, surveyed_on, surveyed_by } => Absent,
    RegionUnsurveyed { obligation } => Absent,
  }
}

// THE CORRIDOR ROW, AND THE RATIO THAT SHOULD WORRY A READER MOST. The facility rows below are the
// densest colocation market in the region, and almost none of them publishes a cabinet density
// anybody has verified against page bytes. Ask coverage_density_read_count against
// coverage_facility_count for the ratio; this paragraph does not carry it, and the sentence that
// used to sit here transcribed the very numeral it claimed the fold derived - review 62961 caught
// that, one paragraph above the edit that removed the transcribed counts for the same reason.
// That ratio, whatever it reads today, is the census's real finding about the corridor: the
// facilities are there, the retail density figures are not published, and a planner who wanted a
// dense cabinet in Hudson-Bergen-Essex would be negotiating rather than shopping.
//
// THE FIGURE IS POST-AUDIT, NOT PRE-AUDIT, and the audit is the part worth reading. This region
// briefly read one more than it does now: a Summit Secaucus
// row citing 12 kW in a 42U cabinet, whose wording reproduced in the page bytes exactly and which
// sat under a block headed "Limited Time: Chicago-Area Facility". Real figure, published price,
// wrong metro. The withdrawal is recorded in
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.citation_verified_against_a_rendering_that_destroyed_its_scope,
// because a citation confirmed by string occurrence alone cannot fail on correct text attached to
// the wrong subject.
//
// THE NUMERALS THAT REMAIN BELOW ARE HISTORY, NOT MEASUREMENT, and the distinction is what decides
// whether prose rots. A sentence asserting what the corpus reads TODAY is falsified by the next row
// that lands, so it must name the producer instead. A sentence recording what was once typed, what
// it was corrected to, and why, is a receipt about a past event: no future row can make it wrong,
// and dropping its numerals would destroy the only evidence that the derivation ever caught
// anything. So the paragraphs below keep theirs deliberately.
//
// THE COUNTS ARE GONE AND THE ROWS ARE HERE INSTEAD, which is review 62780's finding fixed at the
// root: 28 and 1 were hand-transcribed measurements of the repo's own live corpus, and DESIGN
// section 6 says a measurement is cited by naming the producer that re-derives it. Deriving them
// immediately proved the point - 28 had been typed where the rows said 27, because 17 pre-existing
// rows were added as 18.
//
// THE COUNT THEN MOVED TWICE MORE AND NO NUMBER WAS EVER EDITED, which is the property being bought
// and it is worth recording honestly INCLUDING the move that was my fault. It went 27 -> 26 when
// Colocation America NJDC2 was demoted on a reading that the operator named no Secaucus site, and
// 26 -> 27 when that demotion was RETRACTED: the operator names it in a heading of its own, and the
// zero had come from a tag-stripper that destroyed the page it read. So the fold caught one real
// miscount and then faithfully carried one of my bad readings for as long as the bad row existed.
// That is the correct behaviour and the honest summary of it: a derivation is exactly as good as its
// rows, and its value is that a wrong row is the ONLY thing that has to be fixed - there was never a
// second number sitting somewhere going stale while the roster changed under it.
//
// THE WITNESS WAS GREEN BESIDE THAT ERROR FOR AS LONG AS IT EXISTED. It asserted that read
// densities never exceed facilities, and 1 <= 28 is exactly as true as 1 <= 27. Choosing a relation
// over a literal was right - a witness repeating a hand-authored number it cannot re-derive proves
// only that the file was not edited - and the relation chosen was too weak to discriminate. The
// answer is neither a literal nor a weak relation but derivation, which is what these rows are.
//
// TWO MEMBERSHIP DECISIONS A READER SHOULD NOT HAVE TO INFER. digital_realty_ewr10 IS listed even
// though its operator no longer does: the field says facilities MODELLED, and a roster that
// silently disagreed with the corpus would be a second authority on what the census contains. Its
// withdrawal is recorded where it belongs, on the row itself, and a consumer choosing quote targets
// must read that rather than this length. And the out-of-corridor rows in these same modules -
// digital_realty_ewr20 in Clifton, three_sixty_five_bridgewater, h5_philadelphia,
// colocation_america_njdc1, databank_lga4_orangeburg - are deliberately absent: this row is a
// region, not an operator inventory.
//
// THE DENSITY ROSTER OBEYS THE SAME RULE, AND THE ROW THAT TAUGHT IT IS BACK. An earlier revision
// removed colocation_america's density because that operator had no facility beside it - correct
// reasoning on a false premise, since the facility had itself been deleted on a measurement taken
// through a stripper that destroyed the page it read. The operator names NJDC2 at 275 Hartz Way,
// Secaucus in a heading of its own, so the facility is restored and its density with it. The rule
// the episode established still stands and is worth keeping: a density whose facility this region
// does not model is an orphan, because a density is a claim ABOUT a facility and joins to nothing
// without one.
//
// THAT RULE IS DILIGENCE AND NOT A CHECK, WHICH THIS PARAGRAPH CLAIMED THE OPPOSITE OF UNTIL
// review 62980. It read "it is now enforced by the two lists agreeing on operators". Nothing joins
// them: operator_label is never read by any witness, and the only relation over these rows,
// density_reads_never_exceed_facilities, compares two integers - twenty-one against twenty-seven
// holds no matter WHICH operators those are. Re-adding a density whose facility had been removed,
// the exact episode narrated above, still counts and still greens.
//
// AND THE INSTRUMENT IS NOT MERELY UNWRITTEN, IT IS UNAUTHORABLE HERE, which is the part that makes
// the sentence worse than absent rather than merely early. No arm of CabinetDensityClaim carries an
// operator, a facility or any identity - the arms hold figures, wording, citations and obligations -
// so a claim cannot be joined to a facility at all from this side. Writing the join today would mean
// inventing an identity for each claim from its declaration name, which is the positional citation
// DESIGN section 3 refuses.
//
// DESIGN section 4b(1) is explicit that a plan or a note establishes nothing and that reporting a
// rung above the executed evidence is inflation - the failure being that an inflated class never
// ranks for climbing. This one had stopped ranking: the note read like a discharged obligation, so a
// reader who trusted it would have looked for the join rather than for its absence. TRIGGER, as a
// capability: rows carrying a DeclarationRef identity, so membership can be an identity join rather
// than a count comparison - which is what DESIGN section 5 means by "Completeness is an identity
// join, not a count equality". Until then this is a rule the author applies and the corpus does not.
data nj_hudson_bergen_essex_corridor: RegionCoverage = RegionCoverage {
  region_label: "NJ Hudson-Bergen-Essex corridor",
  region_code: "NJ",
  outcome: FacilitiesModelled {
    facilities: [
      equinix_ny1, equinix_ny2, equinix_ny3, equinix_ny4, equinix_ny5, equinix_ny6, equinix_ny7,
      coresite_ny2, coresite_ny3,
      centersquare_weehawken, centersquare_ewr5_secaucus,
      colocation_america_njdc2_secaucus,
      digital_realty_ewr10,
      three_sixty_five_carlstadt, three_sixty_five_weehawken,
      dataverge_jersey_city,
      evocative_ewr1,
      summit_secaucus,
      h5_secaucus,
      hivelocity_jfk1,
      interserver_secaucus, interserver_teb4_secaucus,
      halsey_165_newark,
      databank_ewr1_newark,
      lumen_newark_165_halsey, lumen_jersey_city, lumen_weehawken,
    ],
    densities: [
      equinix_ny1_density, equinix_ny2_density, equinix_ny3_density, equinix_ny4_density,
      equinix_ny5_density, equinix_ny6_density, equinix_ny7_density,
      coresite_secaucus_density,
      centersquare_cabinet_density,
      digital_realty_cabinet_density,
      three_sixty_five_cabinet_density,
      dataverge_cabinet_density,
      evocative_ewr1_density,
      summit_secaucus_density,
      h5_secaucus_density,
      hivelocity_jfk1_density,
      interserver_cabinet_density,
      halsey_165_cabinet_density,
      databank_ewr1_density,
      colocation_america_cabinet_density,
      lumen_cabinet_density,
    ],
  },
}

data southern_new_jersey: RegionCoverage = RegionCoverage {
  region_label: "Southern New Jersey",
  region_code: "NJ",
  outcome: SurveyedNoRetailOperator {
    searched: [
      "Burlington County", "Camden County", "Gloucester County", "Atlantic County",
      "Cumberland County", "Salem County", "Cape May County",
    ],
    causes: [
      ResolvedToAggregatorsOnly { detail: "every search hit resolved to a directory aggregator or a lead-generation page rather than an operator publishing a facility" },
      ResolvedToResellersWithoutFacility { detail: "managed-service resellers with no facility of their own" },
      DirectoryClaimContradictedByOperator { detail: "TierPoint is directory-cited in New Jersey but its own location pages carry NO New Jersey site; Agile Data Sites is directory-cited for a Monmouth Junction facility and now redirects to databridgesites.com, which lists Silver Spring and Aurora and no New Jersey location" },
    ],
    surveyed_on: "2026-09-08",
    surveyed_by: "census lane: NJ central and south",
  },
}

data hudson_valley: RegionCoverage = RegionCoverage {
  region_label: "Hudson Valley proper",
